Each episode in this series will explore one crucial topic and share actionable insights from top subscription app operators.

9
00:00:57,280 --> 00:01:01,600
With me today, Sarah Grana, who works on revenue strategy at Usician.

10
00:01:01,840 --> 00:01:17,200
On the podcast, I talk with Sarah about the cost of not tracking your experiments and decisions, how refunds and chargebacks quietly erase your paywall wins, and why stacking AB test wins should compound your growth, but almost never does.

11
00:01:17,520 --> 00:01:20,320
Hey, Sarah, than thank you so much for joining me on the podcast today.

12
00:01:20,880 --> 00:01:22,000
Thanks for having me, David.

13
00:01:22,720 --> 00:01:27,280
So you spent almost seven years at Babbel and recently transitioned to Usician.

14
00:01:27,920 --> 00:01:36,160
And one of the things you told me when we were preparing for this was that the first thing you did at Usician was you asked, like, where's your log of experiments?

15
00:01:36,320 --> 00:01:37,840
Where's your log of business decisions?

16
00:01:38,640 --> 00:01:42,320
I don't think a lot of companies keep that kind of record.

17
00:01:43,360 --> 00:01:47,680
So why was that the first thing you asked and how do you recommend doing that?

18
00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:57,520
So when I started a company and also when I started at Babbel in my role in revenue strategy, I really looked at, okay, what is the map?

19
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:00,160
of our revenue over the years.

20
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:03,600
So from your revenue, we come from a subscription business.

21
00:02:03,920 --> 00:02:18,000
You can either get money from people that never had a subscription and start a new subscription, people that upgraded from a subscription to another, people that renew a subscription or people that used to have a subscription, then churn and then came back.

22
00:02:18,320 --> 00:02:25,440
So like these four buckets, you have like these four buckets, having the history of how these four buckets evolve can also tell you a lot.

23
00:02:25,680 --> 00:02:39,440
So when sometimes you cannot find experiments or whatnot, you can see, oh, I see that from February 23, all of a sudden the new subscriber revenue went really, you know, like really big.

24
00:02:39,680 --> 00:02:40,240
Like what happened?

25
00:02:40,400 --> 00:02:46,160
And they're like, oh yeah, this is when we started the lifetime subscription or, you know, like this type of thing.

26
00:02:46,400 --> 00:02:52,320
So sometimes it's about finding, yeah, it's great if you have a lock and then you can go through experiments, but you need to differentiate.

27
00:02:52,320 --> 00:02:58,000
Okay, what is important versus not because sometimes you run like some companies run a lot of experiments.

28
00:02:58,160 --> 00:03:00,720
So it's not really useful to go through all of it.

29
00:03:01,280 --> 00:03:09,680
So I would also recommend, let's map the revenue, understand what are the big difference that you see and then try to make okay something happened here.

30
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:10,560
What happened?

31
00:03:11,040 --> 00:03:15,040
And then that will tell you a lot about the history and how things.

32
00:03:15,120 --> 00:03:19,760
things work together also in that particular company or sector.

33
00:03:20,160 --> 00:03:30,240
How would you recommend actually tracking that or like in your ideal state is that just in a Notion dock and a Google dock or is it in a spreadsheet and like the revenue changes that happen?

34
00:03:30,560 --> 00:03:33,040
Like what's the ideal state of something like that look like?

35
00:03:33,440 --> 00:03:54,480
Basically the revenue bucket so to say to me is like Excel, like I would track in Excel like how how are we moving and then from that maybe have a little presentation for me or like then to share and then you map okay here you know this line this happened because of this thing and then you can like link to whatever data take or do whatever documentation that is.

36
00:03:55,040 --> 00:04:00,320
just the company, or you can put, like, within the same Excel file, if you stay in Excel, the links of what happened when.

37
00:04:00,880 --> 00:04:06,800
But yeah, I mean, I'm a bit of an Excel person, but yeah, like everyone needs to find their own way.

38
00:04:07,520 --> 00:04:21,680
When you're trying to optimize conversion retention, when you're working on, as you do, revenue strategy in a company, all these little things add up to become the product in a way that is hard to untangle from each other.

39
00:04:22,480 --> 00:04:28,560
And so having this record means that you know when you ran this experiment, you can then go back and look at that cohort.

40
00:04:28,800 --> 00:04:30,720
Did it churn at a higher rate?

41
00:04:31,120 --> 00:04:34,960
Did they engage in the app app differently?

42
00:04:35,600 --> 00:04:45,280
And so all of that leads to kind of making better decisions over the long haul and being able to look back historically and understand those decisions and maybe retest some of the assumptions from the past.

43
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:50,160
But one of the big things is conversion over optimization.

44
00:04:50,480 --> 00:05:01,040
I think a lot of apps are falling into this trap these days of getting too focused on early funnel metrics at the expense of down funnel metrics.

45
00:05:01,120 --> 00:05:03,120
So what are some of the fallouts you've seen in that?

46
00:05:03,760 --> 00:05:07,280
I think there's two major things that I keep seeing over and over again.

47
00:05:07,760 --> 00:05:14,400
One of them is this not allowing enough time for the cohort to evolve and see what happens later, right?

48
00:05:14,640 --> 00:05:19,360
What you mentioned, like what happens with the renewal rates, like, okay, we have a price increase.

49
00:05:19,520 --> 00:05:21,360
Oh great, you know, we did amazing.

50
00:05:21,600 --> 00:05:22,080
We've rolled this.

51
00:05:22,640 --> 00:05:31,840
And then six months later, you look at the cohort and say, oh actually, the control group actually is performing better than the test group because they are, you know, renewable.

52
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:36,080
So one of them is like this, not looking, you know, like giving the time.

53
00:05:36,320 --> 00:05:38,080
Sometimes it's fine to roll out, right?

54
00:05:38,400 --> 00:05:38,720
It's fine.

55
00:05:38,880 --> 00:05:42,480
You have a win, you roll, but then look back, right?

56
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:49,600
And then the second one is within the same moment in time, like the same snippet in time, but not looking at the right metrics.

57
00:05:49,680 --> 00:05:51,360
Like there's times that we forget.

58
00:05:51,680 --> 00:05:52,640
to look at some metrics.

59
00:05:52,800 --> 00:05:54,400
I give you like a really clear example.

60
00:05:55,200 --> 00:06:00,880
Sometimes with a winback campaign, okay, or like people that cancel their auto renewal.

61
00:06:01,120 --> 00:06:03,280
Okay, when they do that, we're going to send an offer, right?

62
00:06:03,440 --> 00:06:04,640
Okay, they cancel it to renew.

63
00:06:04,720 --> 00:06:06,640
We send an offer because then maybe we get them back.

64
00:06:06,800 --> 00:06:07,120
Okay, great.

65
00:06:07,280 --> 00:06:07,760
We do this.

66
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:09,120
Great, amazing numbers.

67
00:06:09,440 --> 00:06:16,720
Okay, you look a bit deeper and you realize, oh, a lot of people are canceled, you know, like putting their auto renewal off right at the beginning.

68
00:06:17,040 --> 00:06:20,720
And what they're doing is they're asking for a refund and then they're getting the offer.

69
00:06:21,200 --> 00:06:24,000
So you're actually like net negative.

70
00:06:24,240 --> 00:06:31,840
So I think that's also something that tends to happen especially with refunds and charge bags and because people tend to forget about those., like they never happen.

71
00:06:32,640 --> 00:06:38,240
And yeah, you might be even without waiting in time, you might be messing up with your system.

72
00:06:38,800 --> 00:06:44,640
So you really need to understand your whole set of metrics and how they all work together.

73
00:06:45,360 --> 00:06:49,440
Because in most of the cases, something goes up and something goes down.

74
00:06:49,920 --> 00:06:52,800
And you need to make sure you know what's going on.

75
00:06:53,280 --> 00:07:01,440
I hear this all the time with price testing where you double the price and you exactly match the revenue.

76
00:07:01,600 --> 00:07:02,800
So conversion cuts in half.

77
00:07:03,520 --> 00:07:09,840
Sometimes you'll get that win where you double the price and you do get a 25% lift in average revenue per user or something like that that.

78
00:07:10,480 --> 00:07:13,120
But you always have to be looking for those downstream things.

79
00:07:13,520 --> 00:07:15,600
As you said, one thing goes up, another goes down.

80
00:07:15,920 --> 00:07:26,240
You know, when you look at the entire life cycle of a subscriber, any one movement here can have downstream effects if you're not really carefully looking for it.

81
00:07:26,560 --> 00:07:32,960
And I think we're just in this mode as an industry of chasing payback as quickly as possible.

82
00:07:33,440 --> 00:07:36,320
And sometimes it's like it's just a business decision you have to make.

83
00:07:36,480 --> 00:07:46,720
You have to make that decision of like, we're going to sacrifice long term revenue to be able to hit that ROAS at day whatever, seven, thirty, ninety, whatever you're targeting.

84
00:07:46,960 --> 00:07:47,920
But I think what's really important.

85
00:07:48,400 --> 00:07:51,440
and what you're hinting at is like, you got to know what you're sacrificing.

86
00:07:51,680 --> 00:07:56,960
You got to know as you push this number up, which number is going down and are you willing to make that trade?

87
00:07:57,520 --> 00:08:02,800
Because a lot of times people are not tracking those down funnel numbers and don't know the trade they're making.

88
00:08:03,120 --> 00:08:10,880
What are some of the specific examples you've seen of that kind of over optimization and how it impacts the long term?

89
00:08:11,440 --> 00:08:17,440
It happens also a lot when introducing new plans, like when you didn't have the yearly subscription or when you didn't have the lifetime.

90
00:08:17,680 --> 00:08:22,320
I mean lifetime is a huge example because the value that you get from the GERGO is really high.

91
00:08:23,040 --> 00:08:32,880
And then every time, like every time you do a test like lifetime versus something else., like always, the something else is going to lose because people are not looking at what is the LTV of that something else, right?

92
00:08:33,120 --> 00:08:37,920
Like if they would have bought a total subscription, they are not bringing you the 100 euros.

93
00:08:38,080 --> 00:08:39,920
They are bringing you like 300 euros.

94
00:08:40,160 --> 00:08:44,960
So don't compare it with the, you know, 250 compared to 300 and things like that.

95
00:08:45,120 --> 00:08:53,600
So I think like every time there's like a new plan that always like people please look at the lifetime value that this plan would have had.

96
00:08:54,160 --> 00:09:01,360
And then another thing that happened, I said before, like a lot like refunds and charge back of something that people tend to forget about.

97
00:09:01,840 --> 00:09:06,080
So if you are introducing a new payment method and things like this, also like.

98
00:09:06,080 --> 00:09:09,920
Also, especially like, look at those metrics, look what's happening there.

99
00:09:10,480 --> 00:09:15,680
Any other specific places you think people should be paying especially close attention to?

100
00:09:15,920 --> 00:09:34,160
I know, you know, paywall optimization is a huge thing and I've been frankly surprised how big the moves folks have been able to see on the paywall, like over this past year, one of the really big paywall optimization things that people have been doing is having a toggle to enable the free trial.

101
00:09:34,320 --> 00:09:36,240
So by default, you don't have a free trial.

102
00:09:36,880 --> 00:09:40,800
You, you know, tap a button or slide a slider and it enables the free trial.

103
00:09:41,040 --> 00:09:44,080
Now Apple has started to reject some of that.

104
00:09:44,160 --> 00:09:50,800
So I don't know if that's going to be something that's allowed long term, but I've been shocked at like, you know, what a big lift that can have.

105
00:09:51,200 --> 00:09:55,680
But again, I haven't talked to people who looked at that cohort six months, twelve months down the line.

106
00:09:55,920 --> 00:09:59,920
I mean, we're just now, I mean, I think that started about twelve months ago.

107
00:10:00,080 --> 00:10:02,080
So you're going to start seeing those cohorts maturing.

108
00:10:02,400 --> 00:10:09,040
Any things like other things like that that you've seen that that you saw very specifically lead to downfunnel problems?

109
00:10:09,600 --> 00:10:15,280
Sometimes it's not so much what we said, like downfunnel problem, but more like you're just putting the revenue at the beginning, right?

110
00:10:15,600 --> 00:10:17,440
Is it just more revenue than at the end or not?

111
00:10:17,680 --> 00:10:25,600
But one thing that happens that is really clear, like with churn, people usually tend to think churn is a product thing, like sharing is a product problem.

112
00:10:26,400 --> 00:10:43,520
But most of the time, I mean not most of the time, let's not say most of the time, but a lot of the time, like the commercial strategy that you have, what people buy, when they buy it, at what price they buy it, are going to have such an enormous impact on your renewals and extensions.

113
00:10:44,320 --> 00:10:56,720
And what happens is that people like in the marketing side, they don't really, you know, see in the indexes and then the product side, they also don't have a view on what's happening at the beginning of the fun, in a way.

114
00:10:56,960 --> 00:11:03,040
For example, something that I think is going to happen right now is we have now the web checker right in iOS, you can send people to buy.

115
00:11:03,360 --> 00:11:03,600
in web.

116
00:11:03,920 --> 00:11:10,560
What I've seen in different companies is the web renewal rates are significantly higher than the app rate.

117
00:11:10,720 --> 00:11:17,120
So what is going to happen now is a lot of teams, a lot of product teams are going to be like, wow, like our product is amazing.

118
00:11:17,440 --> 00:11:20,960
We have increased our renewal rates by whatever, 20 percent.

119
00:11:21,600 --> 00:11:33,040
If they were to slice from what is a cohort of users that bought, you know, native in app, what is a cohort of users that bought through web, maybe the renewal rates are flat.

120
00:11:33,520 --> 00:11:37,920
And what just happened is that you are acquiring users through other means.

121
00:11:38,720 --> 00:11:41,120
The same can be said, of course, for the subscription plans.

122
00:11:41,200 --> 00:11:51,520
Like if you have like your share of users that buy a one month subscription is bigger or like it grows for whatever reason, your renewals are also going to get better if you look at the total overall number.

123
00:11:52,080 --> 00:12:01,680
So I think when you're looking at data like cohort of the different users, what they bought when they bought it if discounted or not discounted is also another clear example.

124
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:14,320
Like sometimes you do a discount, you bring a lot of revenue, but then those users, they are not going to renew or sometimes you do a price increase, you bring more revenue because your conversion goes down but not as much as your as the price increase goes up.

125
00:12:14,960 --> 00:12:20,080
But then those users, you have like less pool to upgrade users and things like this.

126
00:12:20,240 --> 00:12:24,800
So there's a lot of different colors to this problem.

127
00:12:25,760 --> 00:12:32,480
So what are the specific metrics and ways you dive into the data to better understand that?

128
00:12:32,720 --> 00:12:38,240
Are you looking at both subscription retention and usage retention of very specific features?

129
00:12:38,880 --> 00:12:41,120
Like how are you watching that entire funnel?

130
00:12:41,280 --> 00:12:42,640
What's your preferred way to look at it?

131
00:12:42,960 --> 00:12:45,120
What are the metrics you most look at?

132
00:12:45,520 --> 00:12:49,520
So what is most important to me is which plan did they buy discounted or not?

133
00:12:50,160 --> 00:12:50,640
And where?

134
00:12:51,040 --> 00:12:52,400
Like web versus app.

135
00:12:53,120 --> 00:12:58,080
Then sometimes in some, and this is something that you need to look for your company, right?

136
00:12:58,240 --> 00:13:02,960
Sometimes the marketing channel my makeup for different users and cohorts, right?

137
00:13:03,200 --> 00:13:04,800
So understand what are the different ones.

138
00:13:04,960 --> 00:13:07,200
And once you have that, then you just look at that.

139
00:13:07,600 --> 00:13:08,640
You just look at that cohort.

140
00:13:08,880 --> 00:13:12,960
And then if you see that the cohort is going up or down, you know, okay, probably it's a product thing.

141
00:13:13,360 --> 00:13:23,360
What usually happens is that this the share for those different cohorts is changing over time and this is what brings you fluctuation on renewal rates.

142
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:29,280
It might be different when you are, yeah, when you're doing specific experiments, you know, that might change that.

143
00:13:29,440 --> 00:13:37,120
But I think it's really always good to look at what are those things and make a difference that are upper funnel as upper funnel as it gets.

144
00:13:37,200 --> 00:13:37,680
So to say.

145
00:13:39,840 --> 00:13:50,960
And then when it comes to when I'm looking at a test that I do in the paywall one-on-out, then I'm looking at a revenue per user, of course, but then I'm also having a look always and refunds a charge back.

146
00:13:51,200 --> 00:13:56,320
So I always wait like a couple of weeks, what is happening there because that's like an immediate effect that you can see.

147
00:13:56,800 --> 00:14:02,880
And then always after some months, recheck the cohort, you know, like what has happened with these people.

148
00:14:03,280 --> 00:14:08,480
Sometimes it's a bit frustrating because you have the majority of users buying a 12-month subscription.

149
00:14:08,560 --> 00:14:11,520
So you won't see it until later on, but maybe there's something that you can do.

150
00:14:11,520 --> 00:14:17,440
If you see that the one month behaving in a particular way, maybe you can assume that the same would happen for the 12 months.

151
00:14:17,920 --> 00:14:33,040
You can also look when you have the result, make some scenarios of renewal rates of those different, you know, if the one month was to renew at this, at this, you know, like ten percent less or five percent more, whatever you want based on the result of your tests, what would happen?

152
00:14:33,600 --> 00:14:41,520
And then you can know once, you know, like you have some like really easy way to check in a way, like in a sense, like you keep an eye.

153
00:14:41,680 --> 00:14:44,640
Okay, if it goes below ten, then I know we're in trouble.

154
00:14:44,800 --> 00:14:47,600
I know that probably the winner is actually the loser.

155
00:14:47,920 --> 00:14:55,760
And it just comes back to what we started with, keeping a detailed history is that when you do run an experiment, do you set like an alarm?

156
00:14:56,160 --> 00:14:58,080
Like, you know, three months from now.

157
00:14:58,720 --> 00:14:59,040
Yeah, yeah.

158
00:14:59,280 --> 00:15:06,560
My Google calendar up there has this experiment recheck with my, like, the data analyst.

159
00:15:06,720 --> 00:15:06,800
Yeah.

160
00:15:07,200 --> 00:15:07,600
Gotcha.

161
00:15:07,920 --> 00:15:10,880
So you're looking back at specific intervals.

162
00:15:11,200 --> 00:15:13,680
And do you do that for, I guess you wouldn't do that for every test.

163
00:15:14,080 --> 00:15:17,600
What's the criteria for the things that you're most careful to look back on?

164
00:15:18,080 --> 00:15:34,960
I think like anything that is price changes, I really care about because, for example, if it's about, oh, the layout is different or like the copy is different, then sure, maybe there's a difference in renewal by why would it, right?

165
00:15:35,440 --> 00:15:37,840
Like, so there's certain things that are like probably not.

166
00:15:38,240 --> 00:15:41,520
So I don't know, like you shouldn't be spending a lot of time.

167
00:15:42,080 --> 00:15:46,000
The thing is also, the more you do it, the easier it gets, in a way.

168
00:15:46,240 --> 00:15:51,120
It's just like another part of the, of the analysis, so to say, it's already set up that way.

169
00:15:51,520 --> 00:15:52,800
So you have already got it.

170
00:15:52,960 --> 00:15:54,800
It's like, okay, you put it back, okay, here.

171
00:15:55,040 --> 00:16:00,800
And sometimes it's even with some tools, it's even there that you can just recheck this test, how it performs.

172
00:16:01,200 --> 00:16:14,720
So it doesn't need to be like really time consuming, but usually everything, everything that is with price or discounting or things like this, I would always recheck after, yeah, depending on what we're selling, you know, like three months, six months.

173
00:16:15,520 --> 00:16:18,320
And then if you have to wait a year, it's yeah.

174
00:16:18,640 --> 00:16:26,720
It seems like maybe the priority would be to make sure you're checking back on the things that made the biggest move.

175
00:16:27,520 --> 00:16:37,760
So maybe there was a copy change on the paywall button that did a 25% lift, which is sounds crazy, but it happens sometimes where you have these like crazy lifts.

176
00:16:38,160 --> 00:16:53,680
And so maybe those kind of things would also kind of fall into that bucket of like anything that made a surprisingly big lift, mark that one to go back on because Especially if then you're not seen because something that happens a lot is, oh, we have all these feriments, all posting.

177
00:16:53,680 --> 00:16:59,520
All positive, but then when you're looking at the numbers, you know, you're still flat, you're still improving, but not that much.

178
00:16:59,920 --> 00:17:03,440
So especially if you see that, then it's time, it's more time to recheck.

179
00:17:03,600 --> 00:17:13,600
Like actually, we'll say if you see that the growth of your company follows all your AB test increases, then it's less of a, you know, like red flag.

180
00:17:13,760 --> 00:17:15,920
Maybe you're like, okay, maybe you don't need to retest.

181
00:17:16,160 --> 00:17:17,360
You seem to be doing fine.

182
00:17:17,920 --> 00:17:24,640
I think that because I think a lot of the times, we're like, yeah, all this, like, you know, I look at the list of your experiments with like five percent temperature.

183
00:17:24,800 --> 00:17:27,280
I'm like, but wait a second, like what?

184
00:17:27,840 --> 00:17:32,560
Why are we not growing, you know, like thirty percent year on year if we have all.

185
00:17:33,120 --> 00:17:39,120
these wins and I think this happens also like with product, like with with, you know, like a new feature or not.

186
00:17:39,280 --> 00:17:44,880
Yeah, this lift, this lift, but then when you look over time, it's not really holding together.

187
00:17:45,360 --> 00:17:48,720
So I think there's also like, I don't know why it happens, right?

188
00:17:49,200 --> 00:17:58,720
